### Adapted from RNS

All entries were read from RNS 1.5.7 (PyPI wheel `rns-1.5.7-py3-none-any.whl`,
SHA-256 0e4aaf8cc47f770da34572d2215b45fea900e104b009551600eb9741a41faddf). The
behaviour was adopted and the Rust is written for this crate; no Python was
translated line by line. The findings are recorded in
`design_docs/2026-10-07_rns_157_comparative_review.md`.

- Resource proofs are PROOF-type packets, and a Node routes them to its sender.
  RNS `Resource.py` 765-777, `Link.py` 1157-1164.
  Targets: `src/resource_transfer.rs`, `src/node.rs`.
- Received bz2 is bounded at 64 MiB by default and cancels with RESOURCE_RCL.
  RNS `Resource.py` 123-125, 368, 698-705, 1090-1100.
  Targets: `src/resource.rs`, `src/resource_transfer.rs`.
- Multi-segment advertisements are refused with a sealed RESOURCE_RCL carrying
  the resource hash. RNS `Resource.py` 155-165, 274-339, 1090-1136;
  `Link.py` 1112-1126.
  Targets: `src/resource_transfer.rs`, `src/node.rs`, `src/endpoint.rs`.
- Link data is proved with the link's keys: the initiator's ephemeral Ed25519
  key, and the responder's identity key, which is verified from link-request
  bytes 32..64. RNS `Link.py` 186-189, 274-285, 326-331, 378-408, 970-992,
  1189-1196; `Packet.py` 338-343, 449-458.
  Targets: `src/link/`, `src/reliable.rs`, `src/endpoint.rs`.
- LINKCLOSE closes a link only when its payload decrypts to the link id.
  RNS `Link.py` 674-683.
  Target: `src/endpoint.rs`.
- The Channel send window is bounded by sequence span as well as by count.
  RNS `Channel.py` 357-369, `Link.py` 1147-1154.
  Target: `src/channel.rs`.
- Buffer consumes only stream-data messages (0xFF00). RNS `Channel.py` 118-128
  and 343-349, `Buffer.py` 151-165. RNS's raise on unregistered types was not
  adopted.
  Targets: `src/channel.rs`, `src/reliable.rs`.
- The IFAC flag is refused on plain interfaces and is never set except by
  sealing. RNS `Transport.py` 1271-1290, 1765-1784.
  Targets: `src/packet.rs`, `src/endpoint.rs`, `src/node.rs`.
- The address book evicts its least recently heard idle peer, and route learning
  and relaying no longer depend on book admission. RNS `Transport.py` 957-978,
  1169-1176.
  Targets: `src/address_book.rs`, `src/node.rs`, `src/endpoint.rs`.
- Path responses are learned but never relayed. RNS `Transport.py` 2303, 2351.
  Targets: `src/node.rs`, `src/endpoint.rs`.
- An Endpoint ignores echoes of its own announces. RNS `Transport.py` 2177, 2211.
  Target: `src/endpoint.rs`.
- Announce freshness is tied to the route: a same-blob announce is a first
  sighting once the route has gone, and a live route admits only a later
  emission. RNS `Transport.py` 957-978, 1086-1090, 2207-2296, 2340-2349,
  3219-3226, 3459-3530, 3729-3742.
  Targets: `src/announce_freshness.rs`, `src/node.rs`, `src/endpoint.rs`.

From the Priority 1 batch (2026-10-08), same source:

- Routes default to one week, capped at 1 day on access-point and 6 hours on
  roaming interfaces, and are refreshed when used. RNS `Transport.py` 126-128,
  155, 957-978, 1406, 1426, 2113; `Interfaces/Interface.py` 45-51.
  Targets: `src/node.rs`, `src/endpoint.rs`.
- Path requests: tagless and repeated requests are dropped, and a local
  destination answers on the requesting interface only. RNS `Transport.py` 195,
  1828-1886, 3295-3296, 3380-3416, 3452-3456.
  Targets: `src/path.rs`, `src/node.rs`, `src/endpoint.rs`.
- Routes and bridges are culled when their interface detaches. RNS
  `Transport.py` 880-881, 975-978.
  Targets: `src/node.rs`, `src/endpoint.rs`.
- A two-generation packet-hash filter, with header-type-2 packets for other
  transports dropped and PLAIN/GROUP hop limits. RNS `Transport.py` 832-834,
  1623-1680, 1944-1961.
  Targets: `src/node.rs`, `src/endpoint.rs`.
- Link MTU is clamped in transit, at the terminus and at the initiator. RNS
  `Transport.py` 2058-2088, 2538-2566; `Link.py` 144-216, 300-312, 366-376,
  391-424.
  Targets: `src/link/`, `src/node.rs`, `src/endpoint.rs`.
- Transit bridges have validated state, a proof deadline, and capacity that
  never evicts a validated link. RNS `Transport.py` 872-955, 2060-2160,
  2605-2671, 3200-3202.
  Targets: `src/link/`, `src/node.rs`, `src/endpoint.rs`.
- Link liveness: measured RTT, keepalives, stale teardown and the handshake
  deadline. RNS `Link.py` 80-106, 204, 245-262, 282-283, 419-438, 516-538,
  652-655, 712-805, 929-946, 1130-1135; `Packet.py` 302, 462.
  Targets: `src/link_liveness.rs`, `src/node.rs`, `src/endpoint.rs`.
- The link MDU formula (431 at MTU 500). RNS `Link.py` 73, 512-514.
  Target: `src/endpoint.rs`.
- Hop-scaled link setup, and path rediscovery when setup fails. RNS
  `Transport.py` 138, 697-725, 2062, 3200-3202; `Reticulum.py` 1818-1846.
  Target: `src/endpoint.rs`.
- Channel retransmission: per-envelope tries, backoff, the retry limit with
  teardown, and window growth with tier promotion. RNS `Channel.py` 200-245,
  300-311, 417-483, 555-570, 605.
  Targets: `src/channel.rs`, `src/reliable.rs`.
- Single-packet proofs (PROVE_ALL/APP), delivery receipts and the reverse
  table. RNS `Transport.py` 154, 159, 740-763, 863-870, 1358-1377, 2104-2110,
  2595-2605, 2724-2761; `Identity.py` 946-957; `Packet.py` 391-539.
  Targets: `src/proof.rs`, `src/endpoint.rs`, `src/node.rs`.
- Identity-key fallback for single packets, and opt-in ratchet enforcement. RNS
  `Destination.py` 502-513, 596-654; `Identity.py` 807-902.
  Target: `src/endpoint.rs`.
- Announces naming a known destination under a different key are rejected. RNS
  `Identity.py` 569-577.
  Targets: `src/address_book.rs`, `src/node.rs`, `src/endpoint.rs`.
- Resource cancel and reject (sealed RCL/ICL matched by hash), an accept hook,
  and `max_response_size`. RNS `Resource.py` 155-165, 652-671, 1090-1136;
  `Link.py` 1035-1126.
  Targets: `src/resource_transfer.rs`, `src/endpoint.rs`, `src/node.rs`.
- Index-based resource parts, window-bounded matching, `segment*74` HMU
  placement and a collision re-roll. RNS `Resource.py` 440-512, 877-901,
  1011-1058.
  Targets: `src/resource.rs`, `src/resource_transfer.rs`.
- Resource metadata (flag 0x20). RNS `Resource.py` 261-272, 707-749;
  `Link.py` 838-846, 899-903.
  Targets: `src/resource.rs`, `src/resource_transfer.rs`, `src/endpoint.rs`.
- Resource proof recovery by CACHE_REQUEST. RNS `Resource.py` 652-671, 773;
  `Transport.py` 2576-2581.
  Targets: `src/endpoint.rs`, `src/node.rs`, `src/resource_transfer.rs`.
- The ratchet lifecycle: an endpoint-owned store, rotation at announce, count
  retention, persistence before advertising, and a signed snapshot. RNS
  `Destination.py` 85-90, 206-242, 285-288, 437-475; `Identity.py` 69-74,
  484-508.
  Targets: `src/ratchet.rs`, `src/endpoint.rs`.

From the Priority 2 batch (2026-10-09), same source:

- Adaptive resource request window, carried on the link. RNS `Resource.py`
  58-100, 191-222, 568-576, 640-646, 849-935; `Link.py` 1257-1266.
- Progress-based resource watchdog: part, advertisement and proof timers;
  duplicate advertisements ignored. RNS `Resource.py` 124-136, 224-240, 577-683,
  744-754, 842-848.
- The exhausted request carries the known parts. RNS `Resource.py` 942-976,
  990-1060.
- Multi-segment resources: segments share the original hash, metadata rides
  only on the first, and the receiver completes at the last. RNS `Resource.py`
  274-339, 392-422, 702-835.
- Requests too large for one packet go as a request Resource. RNS `Link.py`
  473-510, 885-895, 999-1000, 1036-1043.
- Resource part size, codec details (split flag, exhausted marker) and metadata
  on the Node path. RNS `Resource.py` 261-272, 343-344, 707-749, 1006, 1257-1330;
  `Reticulum.py` 151-152.
- Address-book and received-ratchet persistence with usage-based retention. RNS
  `Identity.py` 64-75, 100-349, 410-482.
- Announce rebroadcast: a jittered table with one retry, local-rebroadcast
  suppression, a per-interface announce airtime cap with queueing, and relaying
  on the ingress interface. RNS `Transport.py` 124-132, 765-829, 1440-1447,
  1522-1585, 2180-2203, 2338-2386; `Interfaces/Interface.py` 391-427;
  `Reticulum.py` 111-114. The table is bounded, unlike RNS's.
- Packet and name checks: an empty data field, link request and proof lengths
  and modes, the hop ceiling, PLAIN/GROUP announces, and dotted names. RNS
  `Packet.py` 259-275; `Link.py` 149, 187, 225-227, 367, 396-405, 938-941;
  `Transport.py` 1356, 1653-1668, 2211, 2573-2593; `Destination.py` 104-107,
  151.

From the LXMF parity batch (2026-10-09), same source:

- Link data received through a resource session can be proved by the
  application (PROVE_APP). RNS `Link.py` 945-967; `Packet.py` 338-343.
  Target: `src/endpoint/resource_session.rs`.
- Link data sends can wait for their proof, or for a data answer; the timeout
  is max(6 x RTT, 1 s), the floor standing in for RNS's one-second receipt
  polling. RNS `Packet.py` 338-347, 413-417, 444-475; `Link.py` 81-82,
  378-389, 1192-1196; `Transport.py` 252, 740-763.
  Target: `src/endpoint/link_receipt.rs`.
- Sealing to a destination's advertised ratchet, and opening with retained
  ratchets before the identity key unless ratchets are enforced, as one seam
  used by single packets and by applications. RNS `Destination.py` 596-651;
  `Identity.py` 852-908. Target: `src/endpoint/sealing.rs`.
- Announce app data may be rebuilt at each emission, including path responses.
  RNS `Destination.py` 290-296, 678-686.
  Targets: `src/endpoint/registration.rs`, `src/endpoint/paths.rs`.

## Prns

Reference withdrawn September 26 over unresolved provenance concerns. Existing
attribution and license text remain for retained material; they do not verify
upstream authorship. See the repository's Prns donor ledger.

`src/artifact.rs` and `src/msgpack.rs` implement the RNS signed-artifact
envelope (`.rsg` / `.rsm`). Its layout was read from Prns
(<https://github.com/KenAKAFrosty/Prns>), a Rust Reticulum
implementation, at commit `72b6b30d27cac910ce20d370e1dc711fe9b95955` (v0.3.4).
Prns is dual-licensed MIT OR Apache-2.0; Retinue elects **MIT**.

The Rust in those two files was written for this crate and shares no text with
Prns. What was taken is the structure of the envelope: which fields exist, what
they are named, that the metadata map opens with `signer` and `pubkey`, and
that the signature covers the encoded envelope rather than the message. That is
a description of an RNS wire format, and the vectors proving Retinue reproduces
it were captured independently by running RNS 1.4.2's own `rnid` executable
(`oracle/capture_signed_artifact.py`).

The historical test quoted Prns's 224-byte published constant. That comparison
was retired on September 26; current captures use project-selected inputs with
RNS 1.5.2. The original captures remain in the receipt archive and Git history.
